1790668119.001	
1790668119.001	In ancient Mesopotamia and Egypt, rudimentary forms of stamping and carving were used for administrative records. However, true textual mass production remained elusive until more sophisticated techniques emerged in Asia. The development of woodblock printing (xylography) is perhaps the most significant early precursor to the European press. In China, as far back as the Tang Dynasty (7th-10th centuries CE), woodblocks were carved with text and images, inked, and pressed onto paper or silk. These blocks were then used repeatedly to print entire pages or books.
1790668119.001	
